WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.100
Thirteen real-life years have passed&nbsp;
between the release of "Avatar" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.000
its sequel, "The Way of Water." For the&nbsp;
characters, the gap was a little longer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.060
around 14 years. What have&nbsp;
they been up to in that time?

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.440
By the end of "Avatar," Jake Sully had&nbsp;
joined the Na'vi cause and transferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:20.580
his consciousness to a Na'vi body. He&nbsp;
and his beloved Neytiri have managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.460
to overcome their differences and&nbsp;
agree to start a life together.

00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:26.039
"I see you."

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.470
"I see you."

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.560
They're still together roughly 14 years later,&nbsp;
and they've had a few kids. There's their oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.800
son Neteyam, middle child Lo'ak, and youngest Tuk.&nbsp;
Neteyam and Lo'ak are teenagers who are relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.360
close in age. They're grown-up enough that Jake&nbsp;
trusts them to go on missions with him against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.760
the Sky People. Tuk is a bit younger, at about 8&nbsp;
years of age during the main events of the film.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.880
Jake and Neytiri also have&nbsp;
an adopted daughter, Kiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.320
played by Sigourney Weaver. Weaver appeared&nbsp;
in the first film as Dr. Grace Augustine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.940
who died in the conflict between the Na'vi&nbsp;
and the humans. Augustine's avatar body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.520
which was rendered brain-dead, was pregnant with&nbsp;
Kiri at the time. She's about 14 years old in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.380
"The Way of Water," so her birth most likely&nbsp;
occurred shortly after the events of "Avatar."

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.160
"The Way of Water" dedicates a ton&nbsp;
of screentime to these four children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.614
and it's clear future sequels are&nbsp;
going to develop them even more.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.700
At a "Way of Water" press event, Sam Worthington&nbsp;
revealed that director James Cameron gave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.920
a script for a project that's become known as&nbsp;
"Avatar 1.5" to help him prepare to play Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.745
again. It was Cameron's original plan for a&nbsp;
sequel to "Avatar." Worthington explained,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.420
As we see in "The Way of Water,"&nbsp;
human military forces have only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.800
returned to Pandora within the last year.&nbsp;
So in the meantime, Jake and Neytiri have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.880
kept busy with Pandora business, with their&nbsp;
tribe battling other factions on the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.700
getting themselves ready for&nbsp;
the return of the Sky People.

00:01:57.101 --> 00:01:58.121
"What's our plan?"

00:01:58.121 --> 00:02:03.060
Maybe one day, we'll actually see&nbsp;
"Avatar 1.5" come to fruition on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.080
the big screen. "Star Wars" constantly&nbsp;
jumps around in its timeline, after all.

00:02:08.332 --> 00:02:11.640
​​It may have taken more than a decade&nbsp;
for the military to return to Pandora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.900
but they came back with some new tricks&nbsp;
up their sleeves. The most prominent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:21.060
these is that Colonel Quaritch and some of his&nbsp;
compatriots have returned as avatars. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.320
they were killed in the first "Avatar," their&nbsp;
memories have been uploaded to the new bodies.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.600
They even came up with a catchy name for the&nbsp;
project, which you can see on the avatars' vests:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.340
Project Phoenix. The Phoenix, of course,&nbsp;
is the mythological bird who constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.520
dies and returns to life. Theoretically, what's&nbsp;
stopping the military from continuing to upload&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.020
memories into new bodies, practically&nbsp;
making their top operatives immortal?

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.880
By the time Quaritch and his team arrive on&nbsp;
Pandora, the military already has a full base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.140
set up and operational. They get introduced&nbsp;
to the new way of things this time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.640
and it's clear their Na'vi bodies will be&nbsp;
valuable assets in the hostile landscape.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.440
Of course, there's more to be gleaned&nbsp;
between the events of "Avatar" and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.100
Way of Water" than what's in the&nbsp;
movies themselves. Fans of comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.840
books may know there are various "Avatar"&nbsp;
storylines published by Dark Horse Comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:18.420
one of which is the six-issue series "Tsu'tey's&nbsp;
Path." In the first film, Tsu'tey is a Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:24.060
warrior who is initially betrothed to Neytiri and&nbsp;
wary of trusting an outsider like Jake. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.420
he finds himself mortally wounded&nbsp;
during the events of the film.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.800
Anyone who wanted to believe that Tsu'tey&nbsp;
was more than just someone competing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.480
Neytiri's braid in "Avatar" should absolutely&nbsp;
check out his comic book series. The events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.100
of the comics all take place during "Avatar,"&nbsp;
but they help fill in the gaps to make Tsu'tey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:46.080
a more well-rounded character, up until his&nbsp;
untimely demise at the hands of the Sky People.

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:50.280
Set shortly after the events&nbsp;
of "Avatar," the four-issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.640
Dark Horse Comics series "The Next&nbsp;
Shadow," sees the Na'vi attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.419
pick up the pieces after most of&nbsp;
the human forces leave Pandora.

00:03:57.419 --> 00:04:00.239
"The aliens went back to their dying world."

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:04.920
Jake, meanwhile, comes to terms with his&nbsp;
new life. To really drive this point home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.360
the series starts with Jake going to visit&nbsp;
the grave of his human body. It isn't long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.140
before some of the natural Na'vi are&nbsp;
skeptical of Jake's ability to lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.880
and some even plot to kill him by challenging&nbsp;
him to combat with a poison-laced knife.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.040
Jake catches the knife, allowing the poison&nbsp;
to enter his system. While he searches for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.440
an antidote, he begins having visions of his&nbsp;
previous life, including all of the deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.840
that occurred due to his inaction. It's more of&nbsp;
a spiritual journey for Jake than anything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.220
and he obviously gets the antidote, given&nbsp;
that he appears in "The Way of Water."

00:04:39.794 --> 00:04:44.100
If you want to be fully caught up with the&nbsp;
story of "Avatar" before "The Way of Water,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.300
Dark Horse's "The High Ground," which&nbsp;
is based on a script written by Cameron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.120
is a must-read. It's set just before the&nbsp;
events of "The Way of Water." Jake, Neytiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.900
and their family have lived in relative&nbsp;
peace for years, but Jake has always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.880
been suspicious that the Sky People could&nbsp;
return. And that's precisely what happens.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.640
Humans bring an armada of ships to occupy&nbsp;
Pandora once again. Jake and Neytiri round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.320
up as many Na'vi as possible who are willing&nbsp;
to fight to protect their home. The story helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.760
fill in the blanks at the beginning of "The Way&nbsp;
of Water." Even though we see the humans return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.100
to Pandora in the movie, it skips past how&nbsp;
their presence impacted the Na'vi. The comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.260
offers a better sense of the sacrifices the Na'vi&nbsp;
made to protect their home from the colonizers.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.900
With the comic, diehard "Avatar" fans can&nbsp;
get a clearer picture of what transpired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.040
in the sci-fi franchise, so you can&nbsp;
be ready for "The Way of Water" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.060
anything else James Cameron has up&nbsp;
his sleeve in the planned sequels.

